# Tab walks the focus ring.
#
# The ring has always come out on the first Tab — but until WidgetNext was
# bound to a key it could not go anywhere afterwards. context.h's
# process_tabbing is the only thing that moves focus_id, it moves it only on
# InputAction::WidgetNext, and hanabi's input map (preload.cpp) bound
# WidgetLeft, WidgetRight and WidgetPress and stopped. So the app painted a
# ring around whichever widget UIContext::try_to_grab happened to park focus on
# at startup and left it there: four Tab presses produced four byte-identical
# frames.
#
# That is the worse half of "the focus rings are really bad". A ring that
# cannot move is not a focus indicator, it is a sticker.
#
# HANABI_FOCUS_AUDIT=1 prints the ring's state and the focused widget next to
# the version in the sidebar footer. The widget is named by its first labelled
# descendant, because the focusable thing is the row CONTAINER and it holds no
# text of its own — the same name a screen reader would announce.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":["t2"],"active_tab":"t2","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_FOCUS_AUDIT=1

# At rest the ring is away, and focus is parked on the first view row.
require_thread t2

expect_text "ring off Home"

# Each Tab is one step down the list, and the ring goes with it.
key TAB
wait_frames 6
expect_text "ring on Settings"

key TAB
wait_frames 6
expect_text "ring on Blocked"

key TAB
wait_frames 6
expect_text "ring on Review"

# A pointer press puts the ring away — but focus itself follows the click, so
# the next Tab starts from where the pointer left it rather than from the top.
click_ui sb_settings
wait_frames 6
expect_text "ring off"
